@ [date] 2026-08-19
@ [project] Cantor
@ [scope] provider_free_typed_checkpoint_custody_query_protocol

& [Intent]
  + turn exact in-memory checkpoint custody into a callable typed subroutine surface for a future Cantor host or model tool adapter

& [Work]
  + preserved source and completed SJS review and phase lock before implementation
  + implemented four closed operations with registry snapshot binding request and response commitments
  + separated compact inspection from full host-facing resolution
  + joined typed iteration and terminal resume operations to their existing exact custody functions
  + exposed a bounded strict JSON stdin-to-stdout CLI adapter over the scripted registry
  + rejected malformed command boundaries cross-kind use and response mutations

& [Observation]
  + Cantor now has the minimal shape of a tool subroutine: typed input validated transform typed digest-bound output
  + the protocol does not require modifying llama.cpp because an ordinary host can bridge model tool arguments to this command
  + a registry-root expectation prevents a caller from silently querying a different custody snapshot

& [FacultyAttribution]
  + Honesty separates compact inspection full resolution deterministic resume and actual provider execution
  + Security closes variants roots handles sizes and result bindings
  + Planner assigns one required input/output path to each operation
  + Weaver joins the command surface registry and resume mechanisms
  + Scribe preserves request response and registry identities
  + Refiner withholds large state from inspection and boxes large in-process payloads
  + Observer compares all protocol paths against direct functions under workspace regression

& [Next]
  + publish Slice7C and refreshed evidence manifests
  + perform final provider-free release boundary audit and package a concise host integration example
